from physics_helpers import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'Which particle has no electric charge and is found in the nucleus?','Neutron.','4.4.1.1'),
q(1,'What is the atomic number?','The number of protons in a nucleus.','4.4.1.2'),
q(1,'What is the SI unit of activity?','Becquerel (Bq).','4.4.2.1'),
q(1,'What is an alpha particle?','A helium nucleus: two protons and two neutrons.','4.4.2.1'),
q(2,'Explain what is meant by isotopes of an element.','Atoms with the **same number of protons** but **different numbers of neutrons**.','4.4.1.2','2023 1H Q05.1'),
q(2,'An atom has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. State its neutron number and the number of electrons in its singly positive ion.','- Neutrons = 23 − 11 = **12**.\n- The ion has lost one electron: 11 − 1 = **10 electrons**.','4.4.1.2'),
q(2,'Describe what happens when an electron absorbs radiation and then returns to its original energy level.','- It moves to a **higher energy level** after absorption.\n- It **emits electromagnetic radiation** when returning to a lower level.','4.4.1.1'),
q(2,'State how the mass number and atomic number change during beta-minus decay.','- Mass number **does not change**.\n- Atomic number **increases by 1** because a neutron becomes a proton.','4.4.2.2'),
q(2,'How does irradiation differ from contamination?','- **Irradiation** is exposure to radiation from a source.\n- **Contamination** is radioactive material being present where it is unwanted.','4.4.2.4'),
q(2,'Give one natural source and one human-made source of background radiation.','- Natural: **rocks/radon gas**.\n- Human-made: **fallout from nuclear weapons tests**.','4.4.3.1','2023 1H Q05: background-radiation context'),
q(2,'Why is radioactive decay described as random even though a sample has a predictable half-life?','- The decay time of an **individual nucleus cannot be predicted**.\n- Large numbers follow a predictable overall halving pattern.','4.4.2.3'),
q(3,'An isotope has activity 960 Bq and half-life 4 hours. Calculate its activity after 12 hours.','- 12/4 = **3 half-lives**.\n- 960 → 480 → 240 → 120.\n- Activity = **120 Bq**.','4.4.2.3'),
q(3,'An isotope undergoes four half-lives. State the fraction remaining, the fraction decayed and the ratio of decline to initial activity.','- Remaining = (½)⁴ = **1/16**.\n- Decayed = 1 − 1/16 = **15/16**.\n- Decline : initial = **15 : 16**.','4.4.2.3'),
q(3,'A detector reads 110 counts/s with a source present and 10 counts/s without it. What total reading is expected after two half-lives?','- Initial source count rate = 110 − 10 = **100 counts/s**.\n- After two half-lives, source rate = 100/4 = **25 counts/s**.\n- Add unchanged background: **35 counts/s**.','4.4.2.3'),
q(3,'A nucleus with mass number 226 and atomic number 88 emits an alpha particle. Find both numbers for the daughter nucleus and explain the change.','- Daughter mass number = 226 − 4 = **222**.\n- Daughter atomic number = 88 − 2 = **86**.\n- An alpha particle removes **two protons and two neutrons**.','4.4.2.2'),
q(3,'Explain why most alpha particles pass through thin metal foil but very few are deflected backwards.','- Most of the atom is **empty space**.\n- Positive alpha particles near the **positive nucleus** are repelled.\n- Very large deflections are rare because the dense nucleus occupies a **tiny fraction of the atom**.','4.4.1.3'),
q(3,'Explain why a gamma-emitting isotope with a suitable short half-life can be useful as a medical tracer.','- Gamma can **leave the body** and reach an external detector.\n- The source lasts long enough to complete the investigation.\n- A suitably short half-life reduces continuing activity and exposure afterwards.','4.4.3.3'),
q(4,'Explain why an alpha-emitting contaminant can be much more dangerous inside the body than outside it.','- Alpha has **low penetrating power** and is largely stopped by outer skin.\n- Inside the body it is close to living cells without that protection.\n- It is **highly ionising**, depositing energy over a short distance.\n- This can damage cells and DNA, increasing the risk of mutations and cancer.','4.4.2.4','2022 1H Q07.3'),
q(4,'Compare nuclear fission and nuclear fusion.','- Fission **splits a large unstable nucleus**, usually after neutron absorption.\n- It releases smaller nuclei, neutrons, gamma radiation and energy; neutrons can continue a chain reaction.\n- Fusion **joins light nuclei** into a heavier one.\n- Fusion also releases energy as some mass is converted; it needs conditions allowing positive nuclei to overcome repulsion.','4.4.4.1–4.4.4.2'),
q(4,'A detector measures a source for 60 s and records 3,600 counts. A background test records 300 counts in 60 s. Calculate the corrected source count rate and give two ways to improve the measurement.','- Background rate = 300/60 = **5 counts/s**.\n- Corrected rate = 3,600/60 − 5 = **55 counts/s**.\n- Count for longer or repeat to reduce relative random variation.\n- Keep distance and shielding fixed so geometry does not change the detection rate.','4.4.2.1–4.4.2.3'),
q(6,'Explain how neutron absorption can lead to a chain reaction and how controlling the chain differs from an uncontrolled reaction.','- A large unstable nucleus **absorbs a neutron**.\n- It splits into two smaller nuclei, releasing energy, gamma and **two or three neutrons**.\n- The products carry kinetic energy, which can transfer to thermal stores through collisions.\n- Released neutrons can be absorbed by other unstable nuclei, causing more fissions.\n- In a reactor, neutron absorption by control rods helps keep the chain and energy release at a controlled rate.\n- An uncontrolled multiplying chain allows very rapid energy release. It is the nuclei that split, not ordinary chemical bonds.','4.4.4.1'),
q(6,'Two gamma tracers have half-lives of 10 minutes and 10 days. An organ investigation lasts 30 minutes. Explain how a scientist should choose between them; do not assume the shorter half-life is automatically suitable.','- Gamma is useful because it can leave the body and be detected externally.\n- The 10-minute isotope undergoes **three half-lives**, leaving only **1/8** of its initial activity after 30 minutes.\n- It may be suitable if enough signal remains; otherwise useful images could be lost or a larger initial activity might be needed.\n- The 10-day isotope changes little during the test but may expose the patient for longer unless removed quickly from the body.\n- Compare starting activity, biological removal, detector sensitivity and total dose, as well as half-life.\n- Choose a source that provides an adequate signal with the smallest justified dose, balancing the medical benefit and exposure rather than choosing on half-life alone.','4.4.3.2–4.4.3.3')]
write_bank(4,items,'2022 Q07.3 illustrates linked reasoning about alpha radiation and internal contamination. The 2023 Q05 sequence covers isotopes, radioactivity and background-radiation data. Additional questions cover half-life ratios, nuclear equations, historical evidence, source selection, fission and fusion required by the current topic. This small sample does not establish a ranking of the most frequent individual questions.')
